### An opaque type

C cannot hide **one** member, but it can hide **all** of them. The header only **declares** the struct (gives its tag, no members), and the **definition** moves into `qstr.c`:

```c
// in qstr.h: declared, not defined
struct qstr;

// in qstr.c: the definition, which users never see
struct qstr {
    size_t length;
    size_t capacity;
    char *data;
};
```

A struct that has been declared but not defined is an **incomplete type** (Day 13's `tdbad.c` error). Users can still make and pass **pointers** to it, `struct qstr *`, because every object pointer has the same size; they just cannot look inside. A type used only through pointers, with its contents hidden, is called an **opaque type**. You will meet a famous one on Day 26: `FILE` in `<stdio.h>`, which you only ever use as a `FILE *`.

Users usually get only the header files and the **already compiled** library (the `.o` or library file, not the `.c`), so the details really are out of sight.

